This paper examines a specific system of partial differential equations:
\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
-\Delta u + V(x)u = \frac{\mu}{|x|^{\alpha}}\frac{1}{u^{\gamma}} + \frac{1}{2}w\cdot\nabla u, & u>0, \text{ in } \mathbb{R}^N, \\
-\Delta v + V(x)v = \frac{\mu}{|x|^{\alpha}}\frac{1}{v^{\gamma}} + \frac{1}{2}w\cdot\nabla v, & v>0, \text{ in } \mathbb{R}^N,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where the parameters satisfy $0 < \gamma < 1$, $0 \leq \alpha < N$, and $0 \leq \mu < \frac{N-\alpha}{2- \gamma}$. The vector field $w$ is a bounded, smooth vector field on $\mathbb{R}^N$ that vanishes at infinity, and the potential $V$ is a non-negative, smooth function that also vanishes at infinity. The authors demonstrate the existence of a positive, radially symmetric, and non-increasing solution to this system.
